@ddudt the reasoning is that when pressure is really low, it's not the dominant factor that sets the scale of the force balance. IE, if we go from p=0 to p=1e-6, the equilibrium isn't going to change at all. But the way we plot it, the first will be normalized with magnetic pressure, which will in general be largish, while the second will be normalized by p=1e-6, so the "relative" error will look huge.

Ultimately we are comparing 3 terms: magnetic pressure, magnetic tension, and thermal pressure. Two of these are always nonzero (the magnetic ones) so it seems like they make more sense to use as a characteristic scale.

The impetus for this was I was looking at results from a beta scan and the low beta ones looked way worse than the vacuum or high beta ones, but it was just because the normalization was off. In real units they all looked about the same.

@dpanici can do. Would a kwarg like `normalize_with_pressure` or something work?

